questions.modelAnswer:
The if-elif-else statement tests several conditions in order and executes the block of the first true condition. If none is true, the else block runs.
Syntax:
if condition1:
    block1
elif condition2:
    block2
else:
    block3
Example:
marks = 72
if marks >= 90:
    print('A')
elif marks >= 60:
    print('B')
else:
    print('C')
Output: B

In Python a for or while loop may have an else clause. The else block executes only when the loop finishes all its iterations normally, that is, when the loop is not terminated by a break statement. If break runs, the else block is skipped.
Example:
for i in range(2, 5):
    if i == 6:
        break
    print(i)
else:
    print('No break')
Output: 2, 3, 4, No break.

A nested if is an if statement written inside the block of another if statement. The inner if is evaluated only when the outer condition is true.
Example (largest of three numbers):
a, b, c = 5, 9, 3
if a > b:
    if a > c:
        print('A is largest')
    else:
        print('C is largest')
else:
    if b > c:
        print('B is largest')
    else:
        print('C is largest')
Output: B is largest.

1. if: runs a block only when the condition is true.
if marks >= 40:
    print('Pass')

2. if-else: chooses between two blocks.
if n % 2 == 0:
    print('Even')
else:
    print('Odd')

3. if-elif-else: tests several conditions; the first true one runs.
if marks >= 90:
    print('A')
elif marks >= 60:
    print('B')
else:
    print('C')

4. nested if: an if inside another if; the inner one is tested only when the outer condition is true.
if a > b:
    if a > c:
        print('A is largest')
    else:
        print('C is largest')

In every case the condition line ends with a colon and the block is defined by indentation.

break: terminates the loop immediately.
for i in range(1, 10):
    if i == 4:
        break
    print(i)   # prints 1, 2, 3

continue: skips the rest of the current iteration and moves to the next.
for i in range(1, 6):
    if i % 2 == 0:
        continue
    print(i)   # prints 1, 3, 5

pass: a null statement that does nothing; used as a placeholder.
if x > 0:
    pass

Loop else: a for or while loop may have an else block, which runs only if the loop ends normally without a break.
for i in range(2, 5):
    if i == 6:
        break
    print(i)
else:
    print('No break')   # prints 2, 3, 4, No break

